WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.300
You may think you know why Fortnite is the most popular game in the world,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.900
but you're probably wrong.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.320
Sure, Fortnite is the most mechanically sound battle royale game

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.860
and it's accessible to a younger audience who mostly plays on console.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.400
But what you don't know is that Epic Games has gotten their audience to be constantly thinking, constantly theorizing about Fortnite's many mysteries.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.809
They've captured an entire generation of gamers with an experience

00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:29.740
they've never really had before.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.340
They've given them mysteries to solve and secrets to uncover at a rate that no other game can match

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.560
It's something no other game has ever really.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.640
attempted before, if only because the internet would spoil most of the secrets before they could even be hidden.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.980
So, Fortnite is the most popular game on earth played by everyone from your cousin to Drake.

00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:03.029
But the secret to Fortnite's popularity, isn't that

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.290
celebrities are playing it.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.879
The trick is that it has an engaged audience of young fans who keep coming back.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.560
Every season Epic Games works more weird, new mysteries into their game

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.820
and despite the internet existing to document every new piece of information,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.400
there's still a sense of mystery and wonder about Fortnite.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.320
And what's more amazing than anything about that is that they've almost

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.560
perfectly recreated the feeling of playing video games in the 90s, of sharing theories with friends

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.020
and looking for secrets that might not even be there.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.960
All given to a new generation of players who never had a way to experience that.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.300
For example Fortnite's Season 5 launch put in plenty of new Easter eggs for fans to puzzle over.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.560
There's a golf course, a racetrack, randomized Moai heads and even a Viking ship

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.480
dropped on the map as part of the rifts opening up and dropping things into the game world.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.500
Meanwhile previous seasons saw a meteor hanging overhead that eventually crashed into the map

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.820
and changed the game world, creating Dusty Divot,

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.349
pop rocks and a nigh invincible ice cream truck.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.740
Fortnite has been doing stuff like this for months now

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.720
and I think it's really hard to pretend like this isn't a big part of its success.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.800
Instead of focusing on adding new maps to Fortnite, the way that PUBG has gone,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.580
Fortnite has instead chosen to update its map, adding new secrets and Easter eggs for fans to look for.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.320
And on top of that they've even started hiding things in the real world as part of a

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.660
complicated and still unfinished alternate reality game or ARG.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.300
So, why is this making the game popular?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.600
Well to understand that we have to go all the way back to the earliest days of console gaming.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.749
Video game designers have been hiding secrets or easter eggs in their games for decades going all the way back to

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.629
Adventure for the Atari 2600, released in 1980.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.389
and games have followed that example even, years later. Just look at Call of Duty Black Ops 3,

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.699
which hid a secret zombie attack in Nuketown.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:21.309
But a lot of these modern easter eggs are pretty well documented along with their equal opposites,  secrets that don't actually exist.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.080
See in the '80s, '90s, even the early 2000s, most of the information you were getting about video games

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.860
was from magazines or other kids on the playground and kids just like to make shit up.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.640
Look at the rumor that Mew was hidden under a truck in Pokemon Red and Blue.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.980
Or the quest to find the Triforce in the Legend of Zelda, Ocarina of Time.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.489
Neither thing was actually hidden in those games,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.529
but it didn't stop idiot kids like me who didn't have any information from spending hours trying to find them.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:03.550
That's because we didn't really have the internet and what little access we did have to the nascent internet was full of misinformation and lies.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.650
Just ask people who believed Sheng Long was a real dude or that Sonic was in Melee.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.489
But as you know, they became more organized

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:22.420
It was easier to separate truth from lies, but people still wanted to think theorize about these games constantly.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.520
So marketing companies took advantage of that and started creating a ARG's which, when it comes to video games,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.480
tend to be marketing campaigns that bleed the games into the real world.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.770
A great example is I Love Bees, a popular ARG used to advertise Halo 2.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:43.089
ILB started off as a website about beekeeping that was slowly hacked to reveal codes that led players to pay phones in the real world

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.030
that embroiled players in a plot that made them part of Halo's canon.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:50.080
Players who made it all the way to the end also got an early chance to play Halo 2.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:55.179
So, how do decade old marketing tricks and dusty internet rumors relate back to Fortnite?

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.960
Well, Fortnite's marketing strategy and world development borrow a lot from both.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:04.480
Over the past few months, Epic Games has been constantly updating Fortnite's map.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.200
It doesn't happen every update but it is happening consistently.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:13.060
For example, before the meteor dropped plenty of fans had their own theories about what it would do to the map.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.460
Some right

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.800
"The meteorite will not be hitting Tilted Towers and will in fact be hitting Dusty Depot instead."

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.400
Some... not so right.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.160
"April 31 would be May 1, which is the start of the fourth season.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.480
Which means Tilted Towers is getting yeeted on May 1."

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.100
Then, as a rocket began to launch from the island during Season 4, more theories came out.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:46.300
"The world of Fortnite was in a dome and was being protected. Protected from the outside world, the other universes, Ben."

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.560
This led to rifts in the sky, which of course, led to more theories

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.440
"I've seen a circulating theory going around, so that is what I'd like to share with you guys today."

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:58.800
"The entire Fortnite map, and the Battle Royale game, is actually a simulation."

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.200
And now, things have started coming through the rifts, things that might be from our world.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:16.600
Meanwhile, Epic Games kicked off an ARG ahead of Season 5, that saw fans tracking down various Fortnite objects in our world.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:23.780
It started with a person finding the Durr Burger's mascot in the middle of a desert in California, after the burger itself vanished from the game map.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:30.640
After that, a bunch of people showed up at that location and handed out phone numbers that, when called would give players of code that led back

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.660
to the in-game Durr Burger location.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.540
Around the same time, people started spotting Supply Llamas all around Europe and

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.720
while their purpose is still unclear, that didn't stop anyone from speculating.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:49.100
"Maybe there's an IRL challenge, it is also heavily thought-out to be this next big thing that we are going to find is the tomato head."

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.160
All of this put together paints a pretty interesting picture.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.260
All of the information about Fortnite is there on the internet.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.820
It's not a particularly complicated game and it's not like there's an involved plot.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:03.960
The map is the only thing players really have as an anchor point to Fortnite's world.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.920
By changing it and teasing out those changes slowly with both in-game secrets and out of game ARGs,

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.340
Epic has gotten people to think about their game

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.800
constantly and not just that, they're thinking about it in the way that kids

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.020
used to think about games, before the internet.

00:07:17.119 --> 00:07:22.928
One of the reasons they can do that is because Epic Games spent years making an engine that allowed them to iterate their game faster

00:07:22.928 --> 00:07:25.120
than nearly anything else on the market.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:33.140
"What we think is our critical success is our ability to make gameplay impactful and, just constantly improving the player experience

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:34.660
build to build, week to week."

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:38.600
Changes in Fortnite are measured in weeks, not months, not years.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:42.860
Compare that to PUBG which has been much slower to implement updates.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.260
Part of that is that Epic has far more people working on Fortnite than PUBG Corp has working on their game.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:50.940
But, let's look at an even bigger project, World of Warcraft.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:53.920
Blizzard maintains an enormous team to run that game and while

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.159
I don't want to pretend like I know everything about their engine and development process,

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.280
it takes a while for Blizzard to make changes to WoW.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.159
There's even some content that's more than a decade old that's never been changed.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.840
Epic and Fortnite by extension moves faster than nearly any other game on the market.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:13.000
Which means the secrets that you find in the game today might not be there tomorrow.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.620
And then there are the secrets that move around the map like the randomly spawning Moai heads that always carry gold treasure chests.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:22.380
That means that players are incentivized to hunt for secrets and talk about them

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.660
and as they disappear it muddies the truth of it all, just like those internet rumors from back in the day.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:33.240
"The one thing, that I am almost 100 percent sure will be coming through is going to be

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:40.160
an Egyptian-themed area. And I'm almost also certain that this is going to be happening right around Loot Lake.

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.720
Where you can see this current anomaly appearing through the water."

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:47.340
Fortnite has created a culture of fans who speculate about the game,

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:52.659
discuss their theories and then head out both into the game and the real world to find more things to theorize about.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:58.629
While other game designers are trying to copy Fortnite's mechanics or cartoony aesthetics, looking for the next big hit.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:07.809
Maybe the trick is in replicating the way that Fortnite has players looking for and thinking about

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:10.839
mysteries in an era where games just don't have those anymore.

